{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"id\":\"N01\",\"lines\":\"--n\"}\n{\"id\":\"N01.2\",\"verdict\":\"note\",\"relation\":\"part\",\"focus_ar\":\"وَعَادًا\",\"target_ar\":\"أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ\",\"after\":\"Their deliberate acts are countable units, though the verse does not count them.\",\"reason\":\"عَدّ names tallying individual things, while أَعْمَالَهُمْ names intentional acts; the acts could be counted, a generic link.\"}\n{\"id\":\"N02\",\"lines\":\"---\"}\n{\"id\":\"N03\",\"lines\":\"---\"}\n{\"id\":\"N04\",\"lines\":\"---\"}\n{\"id\":\"N05\",\"lines\":\"-nn\"}\n{\"id\":\"N05.2\",\"verdict\":\"note\",\"relation\":\"complement\",\"focus_ar\":\"وَعَادًا\",\"target_ar\":\"أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ\",\"after\":\"A cadence of recurring times could frame repeated deeds, but no schedule is stated here.\",\"reason\":\"عَدَاد marks something that comes at a known time; أَعْمَالَهُمْ names deliberate acts that might recur within such a cadence.\"}\n{\"id\":\"N05.3\",\"verdict\":\"note\",\"relation\":\"same\",\"focus_ar\":\"وَعَادًا\",\"target_ar\":\"وَكَانُوا۟\",\"after\":\"Both senses touch on events recurring or occurring in time, a broad temporal overlap.\",\"reason\":\"عَدَان is a time or recurring period, while كَانُوا۟ establishes the people in a past state.\"}\n{\"id\":\"N06\",\"lines\":\"---\"}\n{\"id\":\"N07\",\"lines\":\"rr-\"}\n{\"id\":\"N07.1\",\"verdict\":\"reading\",\"relation\":\"same\",\"focus_ar\":\"وَعَادًا\",\"target_ar\":\"مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ\",\"after\":\"The dwellings can be heard as the place to which the people return. Their ruined homes make the return-place echo concrete as visible evidence.\",\"reason\":\"المَعَاد is a place of return or final arrival, while مَسَاكِنِهِمْ names their settled homes; both point to a place of habitation.\"}\n{\"id\":\"N07.2\",\"verdict\":\"reading\",\"relation\":\"complement\",\"focus_ar\":\"وَعَادًا\",\"target_ar\":\"ٱلسَّبِيلِ\",\"after\":\"A way leads somewhere, and the root echo in عَادًا can name the destination of that journey. Their being turned from the path thus sounds like being diverted from its end as well.\",\"reason\":\"المَعَاد means a final destination or return, while ٱلسَّبِيلِ is a way one travels; destination and route complement one another.\"}\n{\"id\":\"N08\",\"lines\":\"r--\"}\n{\"id\":\"N08.1\",\"verdict\":\"reading\",\"relation\":\"image\",\"focus_ar\":\"وَعَادًا\",\"target_ar\":\"مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ\",\"after\":\"The dwellings stand still and unspeaking. The quiet of ruined homes makes the rare silence sense audible beside their settlement.\",\"reason\":\"سُكُوت لا يبدئ ولا يعيد means silence without a word, while مَسَاكِنِهِمْ means settled dwellings and carries the sense of motion coming to rest.\"}\n{\"id\":\"N09\",\"lines\":\"nrrn\"}\n{\"id\":\"N09.1\",\"verdict\":\"note\",\"relation\":\"same\",\"focus_ar\":\"وَعَادًا\",\"target_ar\":\"مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ\",\"after\":\"Repeated habit and settled habitation share an idea of constancy, though the link is broad.\",\"reason\":\"العَادَة is repeated practice that becomes a disposition, while مَسَاكِنِهِمْ names places where people settle.\"}\n{\"id\":\"N09.2\",\"verdict\":\"reading\",\"relation\":\"same\",\"focus_ar\":\"وَعَادًا\",\"target_ar\":\"أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ\",\"after\":\"The deeds can be heard as settled repetitions, the customs one repeats until they become easy. Their beautification then shows how ingrained conduct can be mistaken for good.\",\"reason\":\"العَادَة is repeated action until it becomes an easy disposition, while أَعْمَالَهُمْ is their deliberate conduct; repeated practice and deeds meet directly.\"}\n{\"id\":\"N09.3\",\"verdict\":\"reading\",\"relation\":\"image\",\"focus_ar\":\"وَعَادًا\",\"target_ar\":\"ٱلسَّبِيلِ\",\"after\":\"Repeated habit makes a track. The people have a way before them, yet are turned away from it.\",\"reason\":\"العَادَة is repeated practice that settles into a disposition, while ٱلسَّبِيلِ is a traveled way; the habit can be pictured as a track beside the path.\"}\n{\"id\":\"N09.4\",\"verdict\":\"note\",\"relation\":\"complement\",\"focus_ar\":\"وَعَادًا\",\"target_ar\":\"مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ\",\"after\":\"An ingrained disposition can persist alongside inward perception, a thin but pointed pairing here.\",\"reason\":\"العَادَة is practice repeated until it becomes easy, while مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ names inward discernment; the senses can coexist even when discernment fails to prevent the habit.\"}\n{\"id\":\"N10\",\"lines\":\"---\"}\n{\"id\":\"N11\",\"lines\":\"-n-\"}\n{\"id\":\"N11.2\",\"verdict\":\"note\",\"relation\":\"complement\",\"focus_ar\":\"وَعَادًا\",\"target_ar\":\"أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ\",\"after\":\"Benefit can be a fruit returned from work, though no reward is named in the verse.\",\"reason\":\"العَائِدَة is a benefit that comes back to a person, while أَعْمَالَهُمْ names their deeds; benefit may result from an act.\"}\n{\"id\":\"N12\",\"lines\":\"---\"}\n{\"id\":\"N13\",\"lines\":\"-n-\"}\n{\"id\":\"N13.2\",\"verdict\":\"note\",\"relation\":\"image\",\"focus_ar\":\"وَعَادًا\",\"target_ar\":\"أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ\",\"after\":\"The laboring camel gives a faint image for the people’s works, though no animal is mentioned.\",\"reason\":\"العَوْد can be an old camel retaining strength for repeated travel and labor, while أَعْمَالَهُمْ means deliberate acts.\"}\n{\"id\":\"N14\",\"lines\":\"--r\"}\n{\"id\":\"N14.3\",\"verdict\":\"reading\",\"relation\":\"same\",\"focus_ar\":\"وَعَادًا\",\"target_ar\":\"ٱلسَّبِيلِ\",\"after\":\"The path can be heard as an old, established road. Its obstruction then makes the people’s departure from a familiar course sharper.\",\"reason\":\"العَوْد can mean an ancient road, while ٱلسَّبِيلِ is a way or course; the rare road sense and the plain path sense coincide.\"}\n{\"id\":\"N15\",\"lines\":\"---\"}\n{\"id\":\"N16\",\"lines\":\"r--\"}\n{\"id\":\"N16.1\",\"verdict\":\"reading\",\"relation\":\"complement\",\"focus_ar\":\"وَعَادًا\",\"target_ar\":\"مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ\",\"after\":\"The name ʿĀd identifies the people whose homes remain as evidence. The pairing gives the ruined dwellings a human owner and history.\",\"reason\":\"عاد names the tribe of Hūd in the rare naming branch, while مَسَاكِنِهِمْ means their dwellings; a people and their homes complement one another.\"}\n{\"id\":\"N17\",\"lines\":\"rrr-\"}\n{\"id\":\"N17.1\",\"verdict\":\"reading\",\"relation\":\"opposite\",\"focus_ar\":\"ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ\",\"target_ar\":\"وَعَادًا\",\"after\":\"The name ʿĀd carries a return echo, while the adversary’s root names remoteness. Against the ruin frame, the return echo does not save them from being drawn away.\",\"reason\":\"شَطَنَ means to become distant or make distant, while the root echo in عَادًا can mean return after departure; distance and return pull in opposite directions.\"}\n{\"id\":\"N17.2\",\"verdict\":\"reading\",\"relation\":\"opposite\",\"focus_ar\":\"ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ\",\"target_ar\":\"تَّبَيَّنَ\",\"after\":\"The evidence becomes clear to the audience, while the root of ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ names distance. The verse sets manifest evidence beside the adversary’s drawing people far from the way.\",\"reason\":\"البُعْد والانقطاع is remoteness and separation, while تَبَيَّنَ means becoming clear or disclosed; disclosure counters distance from what is evident.\"}\n{\"id\":\"N17.3\",\"verdict\":\"reading\",\"relation\":\"image\",\"focus_ar\":\"ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ\",\"target_ar\":\"فَصَدَّهُمْ\",\"after\":\"The adversary’s name evokes distance, and the verb says that he turned them away. Together they picture one act of drawing people off course.\",\"reason\":\"شَطَنَ carries the sense of becoming distant, while صَدَّهُمْ means turning them away or obstructing them; the rare root image is enacted in the verb.\"}\n{\"id\":\"N18\",\"lines\":\"---\"}\n{\"id\":\"N19\",\"lines\":\"rnr\"}\n{\"id\":\"N19.1\",\"verdict\":\"reading\",\"relation\":\"opposite\",\"focus_ar\":\"ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ\",\"target_ar\":\"تَّبَيَّنَ\",\"after\":\"The evidence becomes plain while the adversarial root can mean crooked deviation. The clear evidence stands against the bent direction that leads them from the way.\",\"reason\":\"تَبَيَّنَ means becoming clear or disclosed, while شَطَنَ can mean crookedness or departure from one’s intended direction; clarity opposes a bent course.\"}\n{\"id\":\"N19.2\",\"verdict\":\"note\",\"relation\":\"complement\",\"focus_ar\":\"ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ\",\"target_ar\":\"أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ\",\"after\":\"The acts are a possible field for deviation from an intended direction, though no specific deed is called crooked.\",\"reason\":\"شَطَنَهُ can mean turning someone away from their intent or direction, while أَعْمَالَهُمْ names intentional acts.\"}\n{\"id\":\"N19.3\",\"verdict\":\"reading\",\"relation\":\"same\",\"focus_ar\":\"ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ\",\"target_ar\":\"فَصَدَّهُمْ\",\"after\":\"The root sense of deviation is carried into the act of turning them away. The two words make the obstruction feel like a departure from the intended course.\",\"reason\":\"شَطَنَهُ means to turn someone away from their direction, while فَصَدَّهُمْ means to turn them aside or obstruct them; both name diversion.\"}\n{\"id\":\"N20\",\"lines\":\"--rr\"}\n{\"id\":\"N20.3\",\"verdict\":\"reading\",\"relation\":\"opposite\",\"focus_ar\":\"ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ\",\"target_ar\":\"وَزَيَّنَ\",\"after\":\"The foul serpent or plant image gives the adversary an ugly form, while وَزَيَّنَ says that he makes the deeds appear beautiful. An ugly source stands behind a fabricated beauty.\",\"reason\":\"The rare شَيْطَان image can be a قبيح snake or plant, while زَيَّنَ means making something beautiful; ugliness and adornment oppose one another.\"}\n{\"id\":\"N20.4\",\"verdict\":\"reading\",\"relation\":\"image\",\"focus_ar\":\"ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ\",\"target_ar\":\"فَصَدَّهُمْ\",\"after\":\"The repulsive creature image makes the people’s turning away bodily visible. The ugly shaytan and فَصَدَّهُمْ become one picture of revulsion carried into diversion from the path.\",\"reason\":\"The rare sense evokes a hideous snake or plant that repels, while فَصَدَّهُمْ means turning them away; the creature’s repulsion images their diversion.\"}\n{\"id\":\"N21\",\"lines\":\"-r-\"}\n{\"id\":\"N21.2\",\"verdict\":\"reading\",\"relation\":\"part\",\"focus_ar\":\"أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ\",\"target_ar\":\"تَّبَيَّنَ\",\"after\":\"The deeds can carry an echo of applying the mind to something with understanding. That mental effort is one stage by which what is hidden can become clear, though here the clear evidence is the ruins.\",\"reason\":\"أَعْمَلَ ذِهْنَهُ means applying one’s mind with understanding, while تَبَيَّنَ means becoming clear; applying understanding can be part of reaching clarity.\"}\n{\"id\":\"N22\",\"lines\":\"---\"}\n{\"id\":\"N23\",\"lines\":\"r--\"}\n{\"id\":\"N23.1\",\"verdict\":\"reading\",\"relation\":\"complement\",\"focus_ar\":\"أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ\",\"target_ar\":\"وَعَادًا\",\"after\":\"Their deeds can sound like labor for which a return is due. The return echo in the name makes the ruin account carry a faint sense of reckoning.\",\"reason\":\"العَمَالَة is wages or sustenance earned for work, while عَادًا carries a root echo of return; work and what comes back for it complement each other.\"}\n{\"id\":\"N24\",\"lines\":\"r--\"}\n{\"id\":\"N24.1\",\"verdict\":\"reading\",\"relation\":\"complement\",\"focus_ar\":\"أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ\",\"target_ar\":\"وَعَادًا\",\"after\":\"Their deeds can be heard as exchanges between people, each act answered by a return. The root echo of ʿĀd gives those acts a faint reciprocal circuit.\",\"reason\":\"المُعَامَلَة is dealing or exchange between people, while عَادًا carries the root sense of return; exchange and return answer one another.\"}\n{\"id\":\"N25\",\"lines\":\"-n-\"}\n{\"id\":\"N25.2\",\"verdict\":\"note\",\"relation\":\"complement\",\"focus_ar\":\"أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ\",\"target_ar\":\"مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ\",\"after\":\"Hand labor can complement dwellings as the work that builds or maintains them, though the verse does not say who made the homes.\",\"reason\":\"العَمَلَة are people who work with their hands, while مَسَاكِنِهِمْ names dwelling places that can be built by such labor.\"}\n{\"id\":\"N26\",\"lines\":\"--n-\"}\n{\"id\":\"N26.3\",\"verdict\":\"note\",\"relation\":\"complement\",\"focus_ar\":\"أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ\",\"target_ar\":\"ٱلسَّبِيلِ\",\"after\":\"Toil can accompany travel along a way, but no effort on the path is stated here.\",\"reason\":\"التَّعَنِّي is taking pains over a task, while ٱلسَّبِيلِ is a way one travels; exertion can accompany the journey.\"}\n{\"id\":\"N27\",\"lines\":\"---\"}\n{\"id\":\"N28\",\"lines\":\"---\"}\n{\"id\":\"N29\",\"lines\":\"--r\"}\n{\"id\":\"N29.3\",\"verdict\":\"reading\",\"relation\":\"complement\",\"focus_ar\":\"أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ\",\"target_ar\":\"مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ\",\"after\":\"The root’s rare عامل sense can denote a working bodily member, including an eye that watches at a distance. مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ shifts from bodily sight to inward discernment: they had sight, yet went astray.\",\"reason\":\"عَامِلَة can name a working bodily organ, even an eye with far sight, while مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ means possessing inward perception; physical seeing complements inner insight.\"}\n{\"id\":\"N30\",\"lines\":\"-rr\"}\n{\"id\":\"N30.2\",\"verdict\":\"reading\",\"relation\":\"complement\",\"focus_ar\":\"أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ\",\"target_ar\":\"فَصَدَّهُمْ\",\"after\":\"A road already worn by travel is something from which travelers can be turned. The well-used track and their diversion form a concrete scene.\",\"reason\":\"طَرِيق مُعْمَل is a well-traveled road, while فَصَدَّهُمْ means turning them away or obstructing them; the traveled road complements the act of diversion.\"}\n{\"id\":\"N30.3\",\"verdict\":\"reading\",\"relation\":\"same\",\"focus_ar\":\"أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ\",\"target_ar\":\"ٱلسَّبِيلِ\",\"after\":\"The path can be heard as a route already worn by travel. That makes the blocked way feel familiar and physical, not merely abstract.\",\"reason\":\"طَرِيق مُعْمَل is a road made busy by travel, while ٱلسَّبِيلِ is a way or path; the rare route image and the plain path sense coincide.\"}"}}
